{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01MWLrNFgx4gSqu4E28qsMak","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01LRqtvcoERfEwWBa8CsTfAQ","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_11/projects/phase-gated-compound-sparse/artifacts/_project/paper/background.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1524,"cache_read_input_tokens":45953,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1524,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01ETZEAUWwRj8ig45ZUMzJhH","session_id":"55f30410-6db0-48bf-814f-3fb31e0f38c1","uuid":"ad8f5151-da7d-4e0d-8cbb-ee8506184d89"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LRqtvcoERfEwWBa8CsTfAQ","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t% Section 3: Background and Problem Setup\n2\t% Note: eq:ii and eq:gradient are the canonical definitions.\n3\t% Full formulation details in Appendix (app:formulation-details).\n4\t\n5\t\\section{Background and Problem Setup}\n6\t\\label{sec:background}\n7\t\n8\tWe formalize compound sparsity, define the Interaction Index, and introduce phase segmentation. Full mathematical details appear in Appendix~\\ref{app:formulation-details}.\n9\t\n10\t\\paragraph{Compound sparsity.}\n11\tLet $s \\in (0,1)$ denote a unified sparsity budget. \\emph{Attention head pruning} removes the bottom-$s$ fraction of heads per layer by Frobenius norm importance \\cite{voita-acl2019, michel-neurips2019}. \\emph{KV cache eviction} removes the bottom-$s$ fraction of token positions from the cache. We compare two strategies: \\emph{importance-based}, which scores positions by position-normalized cumulative attention mass (Eq.~\\ref{eq:kv-importance} in Appendix~\\ref{app:formulation-details}), and \\emph{random}, which evicts uniformly at random \\cite{nacl-acl2024}. Applying both simultaneously yields \\emph{compound sparsity}; the two axes interact because head pruning alters the importance ranking used for eviction \\cite{spark-aaai2026}. Existing joint-pruning approaches such as GRASPrune \\cite{graspune-acl2026} learn global budget-constrained gates but treat decisions as static across generation.\n12\t\n13\t\\paragraph{Interaction Index.}\n14\tFor a text segment, let $\\text{PPL}_c$ denote perplexity under condition $c \\in \\{d, a, k, b\\}$ (dense, attention-only, KV-only, compound). Normalizing by the dense baseline yields $r_c = \\text{PPL}_c / \\text{PPL}_d$, and:\n15\t\\begin{equation}\n16\t    \\mathrm{II} = r_b - r_a - r_k + 1.\n17\t    \\label{eq:ii}\n18\t\\end{equation}\n19\t$\\mathrm{II} > 0$ indicates super-additive degradation; $\\mathrm{II} = 0$ indicates additive independence. We adopt the additive null as primary: it assumes $r_b = r_a + r_k - 1$ (compound loss equals sum of individual losses), the most conservative independence assumption. Although the two axes are mechanistically linked (\\S\\ref{sec:analysis}), rejection ($\\mathrm{II} > 0$) establishes super-additivity regardless of null specification. Appendix~\\ref{app:multiplicative-null} reports parallel results under the multiplicative null ($r_b = r_a \\cdot r_k$); the phase-severity gradient is preserved ($G_{\\times}{=}21$--$64{\\times}$), supporting null-choice invariance. The II distribution is heavily right-skewed; we report medians throughout.\n20\t\n21\t\\paragraph{Severity gradient.}\n22\tTo capture phase variation, the per-problem \\emph{amplification ratio} is:\n23\t\\begin{equation}\n24\t    G^{(i)} = \\frac{\\mathrm{II}^{(i)}_{\\,\\textit{low}}}{\\mathrm{II}^{(i)}_{\\,\\textit{high}}},\n25\t    \\label{eq:gradient}\n26\t\\end{equation}\n27\twhere $\\mathrm{II}^{(i)}_{\\,\\textit{low/high}}$ is the interaction index in the low/high-sparsity phase for problem $i$. We report $G = \\widetilde{G^{(i)}}$, the median across problems, with bootstrap CIs. We use the median of per-problem ratios (not the ratio of medians) to preserve per-problem pairing; see Appendix~\\ref{app:profiling-subset} for comparison. $G$ serves as an auxiliary descriptive ratio that illustrates the degree of phase concentration; the primary statistical evidence for the severity gradient rests on $\\Delta$ and paired Wilcoxon tests (\\S\\ref{sec:phase-amplification}).\n28\t\n29\t\\paragraph{Phase segmentation.}\n30\t\\label{par:phase-segmentation}\n31\tInspired by ThinKV's phase-aware perspective on reasoning-time compression~\\cite{thinkv-iclr2026}, each generated sequence is partitioned into three phases (low-, mid-, high-sparsity) by tercile boundaries of per-position attention sparsity, using a quantile-based segmentation rather than explicit reasoning delimiters (e.g., \\texttt{<think>} tokens). Low-sparsity phases correspond to broadly distributed attention (exploratory reasoning); high-sparsity phases to concentrated attention (structured computation). We note that this segmentation is an \\emph{operationalization choice}: attention sparsity serves as a continuous proxy for reasoning phase rather than a theoretically grounded boundary. We validate robustness to segmentation granularity (tercile/quartile/quintile; Appendix~\\ref{app:seg-robust}) and minimum phase length (Appendix~\\ref{app:mpl-sensitivity}). Validation against DeepSeek's explicit \\texttt{<think>} markers using adaptive per-sequence quantile segmentation yields substantial agreement ($\\kappa{=}0.88$, AUC-ROC${=}0.98$; $n{=}48$; Appendix~\\ref{app:think-validation}). Details in Appendix~\\ref{app:formulation-details}.\n32\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01ETZEAUWwRj8ig45ZUMzJhH","session_id":"55f30410-6db0-48bf-814f-3fb31e0f38c1","uuid":"9c07b7a1-6553-4d8c-b9d5-828f5eb3f13a","timestamp":"2026-05-16T08:21:43.692Z"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"a829dc5b13ff7bb01","tool_use_id":"toolu_01U2WRc4btzp28LXEEKdHEaT","status":"completed","output_file":"","summary":"R1: Methods + Experiments Review","usage":{"total_tokens":72143,"tool_uses":21,"duration_ms":120691},"uuid":"c78849a1-55b2-46a4-b13c-edcd7f302ea2","session_id":"55f30410-6db0-48bf-814f-3fb31e0f38c1"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01U2WRc4btzp28LXEEKdHEaT","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have a thorough understanding of the paper. Let me now write the review.\n\n---\n\n## Review: \"Phase-Dependent Amplification of Compound Sparsity in Long-Chain Reasoning\"\n\n### Summary\n\nThis paper investigates how compound sparsity (simultaneous attention head pruning + KV cache eviction) interacts with reasoning phases in long-chain generation. The core finding is that compound degradation severity is not uniform but phase-modulated: information-dense (\"low-sparsity\") reasoning phases suffer disproportionately more super-additive degradation than structured computation phases, and this effect is primarily driven by importance-based eviction (15-17x larger gradient than random). The paper proposes \"importance discrimination collapse\" as a correlational mechanism and validates across two Qwen2-architecture models (n=500), with preliminary LLaMA cross-architecture evidence (n=49).\n\n### Strengths\n\n- **Well-defined metric and factorial design.** The Interaction Index (II) cleanly operationalizes super-additivity, and the 2 models x 2 strategies x 4 sparsity levels design provides systematic coverage. The additive vs. multiplicative null comparison is a good robustness check.\n\n- **Strong statistical methodology for the primary claim.** The paper uses appropriate nonparametric tests (Wilcoxon, permutation), paired per-problem analysis, median-based estimators for heavy-tailed data, Bonferroni correction, and Jonckheere-Terpstra for dose-response. The n=500 replication substantially strengthens the n=50 pilot.\n\n- **Thorough robustness checks.** Segmentation granularity (tercile/quartile/quintile), minimum phase length sensitivity, asymmetric sparsity spot checks, and outlier winsorization all support the primary finding's stability.\n\n- **Honest reporting of negative/mixed results.** The pilot mitigation failing its pre-registered gate, entropy redistribution not replicating on Qwen, LLaMA cross-architecture evidence being inconclusive for strategy interaction, and the crossover being model-dependent are all transparently reported.\n\n- **Phase segmentation validation.** The kappa=0.88 agreement with DeepSeek's explicit <think> markers on GSM8K (n=48) provides meaningful validation that attention-sparsity phases track model-identified reasoning boundaries.\n\n- **Appropriate use of effect sizes.** Cohen's dz alongside p-values prevents over-reliance on significance alone.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n- **(Major) Circularity confound acknowledged but not resolved.** The paper notes (Limitations) that importance-based eviction scores positions using cumulative attention mass *from the pruned model*, creating a circular dependency: head pruning degrades the very scores used for eviction. The entire phase-strategy interaction pattern could be an artifact of this scoring confound rather than a property of \"compound sparsity\" in general. The paper's core claim would be substantially weaker if reframed as \"H2O-style scoring fails gracefully in high-sparsity phases but catastrophically in low-sparsity phases when combined with head pruning.\" Testing even one eviction method with pruning-independent scoring (e.g., recency, learned, or pre-computed static importance) would disambiguate this.\n\n- **(Major) Phase classification is a continuous operationalization treated as a finding.** Segmenting by attention sparsity terciles and then measuring how much *compound sparsity interacts with attention sparsity* is partially circular: tokens with broadly distributed attention (low-sparsity phase) are precisely those where eviction of \"unimportant\" positions matters most, almost by construction. The II asymmetry between phases may be a mathematical consequence of the attention distribution's shape rather than revealing something non-obvious about \"reasoning phases.\" The <think> token validation partially mitigates this but only shows correlation between attention sparsity and reasoning boundaries---not that the phase-dependent II pattern is non-trivial.\n\n- **(Major) Mechanism claim is weak (r=0.26 for Gini-II).** The paper acknowledges that both strategies exhibit \"near-identical Gini drops\" yet severity diverges 15-17x, which means Gini collapse cannot be the mechanism. The \"structural specificity\" argument (importance-pruning removes dispersed-attention heads) is plausible but is presented with only correlational evidence (Mann-Whitney on pruned vs. retained head Gini). There is no demonstrated causal path from Gini collapse to the 15-17x severity gap.\n\n- **(Minor) Cross-architecture evidence is underpowered and somewhat contradicts the narrative.** LLaMA (n=49) shows random eviction *also* has a significant phase gradient (dz 0.35-0.44), which weakens the strategy-specificity claim. The paper frames this as \"architecture-dependent\" but the n=49 sample is insufficient to distinguish \"weak effect we can't detect\" from \"absent effect.\" The 14B results and GPQA results appear as placeholders in the paper (Tables 5/6 show \"---\"), making it unclear what is actually demonstrated vs. planned.\n\n- **(Minor) Sample size heterogeneity weakens secondary claims.** GSM8K (n=50), LLaMA (n=49), GPQA (n=50 in the summary but n=198 in the tex), and the pilot mitigation (n=30) are all substantially underpowered relative to the primary n=500 analysis. The GSM8K random conditions all have |d|<0.164, well below detectability (d>=0.40 needed at n=50). Claiming \"strategy specificity attenuates\" on GSM8K when random is simply undetectable is overinterpretation.\n\n- **(Minor) The \"strategy specificity ratio\" metric conflates detection with magnitude.** Ratios of 15-36x on MATH-500 vs. 2.3-3.8x on GSM8K are computed differently: MATH-500 compares two significant effects, but GSM8K compares a significant effect to a non-significant one. Dividing by a near-zero non-significant denominator does not yield a meaningful ratio.\n\n- **(Minor) Greedy decoding only.** All experiments use greedy decoding (temperature=0). Stochastic decoding would alter attention patterns and potentially the phase structure; the findings may not generalize to sampling-based inference.\n\n- **(Minor) max_new_tokens=2048 for the primary experiment is short.** DeepSeek's reasoning traces frequently truncate even at 8192 tokens (76% truncation at s=0.30). The primary perplexity analysis at 2048 tokens may be capturing truncation-related artifacts in the attention patterns of late-generation tokens approaching the limit.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. Have you considered testing a pruning-independent eviction strategy (e.g., pure recency-based or pre-computed static importance from the dense model) to disentangle the H2O scoring confound from the compound interaction per se?\n\n2. If you define phases by attention sparsity and then show that compound sparsity severity correlates with attention sparsity, how do you distinguish your finding from the tautological statement that \"eviction matters more where attention is dispersed\"?\n\n3. For the Gini mechanism: if both strategies show identical Gini collapse but 15-17x severity difference, what fraction of the severity difference does your \"structural specificity\" account for quantitatively?\n\n4. The per-problem concordance across models is non-significant (rho=-0.107). Does this undermine the claim of a universal \"phase-modulated\" phenomenon, suggesting instead that model-specific attention patterns drive the effect?\n\n5. Why were the 14B and GPQA results left as placeholders in the paper? Are these experiments complete?\n\n### Missing Experiments or Analyses\n\n1. **Pruning-independent eviction control.** Testing recency-based or static-score eviction under the same head pruning would isolate whether the interaction is specific to adaptive importance scoring or general to compound sparsity.\n\n2. **Phase-shuffled null.** Randomly permuting the phase assignments within each problem while preserving token-level II values would test whether the phase-II correlation exceeds chance beyond what the attention-sparsity/II relationship mechanically guarantees.\n\n3. **Per-token regression analysis.** Regressing II on continuous attention sparsity (rather than discretizing into phases) would reveal whether the tercile boundaries are special or if this is a smooth monotonic relationship.\n\n4. **Larger model validation.** The 14B and GPQA experiments are incomplete (placeholders). For a paper claiming generality of phase modulation, these should be filled in.\n\n5. **Multiple random seeds.** Random eviction uses a single random draw per condition. Confidence intervals on random eviction should account for seed variability.\n\n### Preliminary Score: 4 (Weak Accept)\n\n**Justification:** The primary finding---that compound sparsity severity concentrates in low-attention-sparsity phases under importance-based eviction---is well-supported statistically at n=500 with appropriate methodology. The paper is thorough in its robustness checks and honest about limitations. However, the circularity concern (importance scoring confound) and the partial tautology in the phase classification substantially limit the novelty of the insight: \"importance-based eviction, which relies on attention scores, fails more where attention is dispersed\" is less surprising than the framing suggests. The mechanism claim (discrimination collapse) is acknowledged as correlational and quantitatively insufficient (r=0.26, identical Gini drops across strategies). The incomplete tables (14B, GPQA) weaken the claimed scope. Overall, this is a solid empirical characterization with useful observations for the KV cache compression community, but the conceptual novelty is limited by the confound structure, and the generalization claims are not yet fully supported.\n\n### Confidence: 4/5\n\nI have substantial expertise in transformer compression and statistical methodology. My main uncertainty is whether the GPQA/14B experiments (described in the review context but showing as placeholders in the actual tex) should be evaluated as \"demonstrated\" or \"planned.\" If demonstrated, the paper is somewhat stronger on generalization; if not, the scope claims are not adequately supported."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a829dc5b13ff7bb01 (use SendMessage with to: 'a829dc5b13ff7bb01' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 73574\ntool_uses: 21\nduration_ms: 120693</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"55f30410-6db0-48bf-814f-3fb31e0f38c1","uuid":"af4071a3-9806-4356-a5aa-5c880e7f4049","timestamp":"2026-05-16T08:22:36.553Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 for a top ML venue review. Your focus: **methods and experiments**. Review the following paper blindly (no knowledge of outcome tiers). Use a 6-point scale (1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept). Target venue acceptance rate ~25%.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** \"Phase-Dependent Amplification of Compound Sparsity in Long-Chain Reasoning\"\n\n**Core claim:** Compound sparsity (simultaneous head pruning + KV cache eviction) severity is phase-modulated: information-dense reasoning phases suffer disproportionately more compound degradation than computation-light phases, regardless of eviction strategy. Strategy specificity (importance > random) is a secondary, conditional effect.\n\n**Models:** DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B (DS), Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct (QW), DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-8B (LLaMA), Qwen2.5-14B-Instruct (QW-14B).\n**Benchmarks:** MATH-500, GSM8K, GPQA Diamond.\n**Sparsity levels:** s ∈ {0.15, 0.20, 0.25, 0.30}\n**Eviction strategies:** Importance-based and Random.\n\n## Key Experimental Results\n\n**Table 1 (Primary, MATH-500, importance, n=500):**\n- DS-7B phase gradient: 0.39 → 1.21 → 3.99 → 8.48\n- QW-7B: 0.07 → 0.31 → 1.07 → 3.16\n- All Bonferroni p < 0.005, Cohen's dz 0.61–1.13 (DS), 0.37–1.44 (QW), Spearman ρ = 1.0\n\n**Table 2 (LLaMA n=250, MATH-500):**\n- Importance dz: 0.92, 1.01, 1.05, 0.97 (all large)\n- Random dz: 0.52, 0.58, 0.65, 0.72 (all medium)\n- Imp–Rand dz: 0.11, 0.14, 0.18, 0.22 (negligible–small)\n- 0/4 Bonferroni significant\n\n**Table 3 (GPQA Diamond, n=50):**\n- DS importance: 0.18, 0.52, 1.35, 2.89; DS random (s025/s030 only): 0.92, 1.85\n- QW importance: 0.02, 0.11, 0.58, 1.42; QW random (s025/s030 only): 0.41, 0.95\n\n**Table 4 (Strategy specificity ratio across benchmarks):**\n- MATH-500: DS 15–36×, QW 2.7–20× (Strong)\n- GSM8K: DS 2.3–3.8×, QW ~2× (Weak)\n- GPQA: DS 1.5–1.6×, QW 1.4–1.5× (Negligible)\n\n**Table 5 (MATH-500 vs GPQA at DS imp s=0.15):**\n- MATH-500: median_diff=0.39, II_low/II_high ratio=66×\n- GPQA: median_diff=0.18, ratio=24×\n\n**Table 6 (14B vs 7B, importance, MATH-500):**\n- DS-7B: 0.39, 1.21, 3.99, 8.48\n- QW-7B: 0.07, 0.31, 1.07, 3.16\n- QW-14B: 0.04, 0.19, 0.72, 1.85\n- Strategy specificity ratio at s025 = 1.89× (below 2× threshold)\n- Dense acc drops from 0.58 to 0.28 at s030\n\n**Table 7 (Gini collapse mechanism):**\n- Imp vs Rand Gini delta: no significant difference (all p > 0.28)\n- Overall Gini–II correlation r = 0.26\n- DS importance Gini–II: 0.22–0.35; DS random: 0.18–0.32\n\n**Table 8 (Language switching, DS MATH-500, importance, n=50):**\n- Switch rate: 0% → 4% → 12% → 26% → 40% (monotonic)\n- CN accuracy ≈ 0; EN accuracy drops from 0.52 to 0.10\n\n**Pilot mitigation (Conclusion):** Oracle phase recovery 18%, heuristic 32%, both p < 0.025 but CIs cross zero.\n\n**Appendix A:** LLaMA n=250 detail — confirms regression to mean from n=50 (dz 0.19–0.34 → 0.11–0.22). GQA distributes head removal impact uniformly.\n**Appendix B:** GPQA has 47% low-sparsity tokens vs MATH-500, explaining reduced strategy specificity.\n\n## Review Context (Experimental Design)\nThe full experimental plan includes these experiments:\n1. c8_llama_n250_fixed — LLaMA n=250 full battery (8 conditions). Paper reports this.\n2. c8_llama_gpu0_s025 — LLaMA s025 single-condition accelerated run. Subsumed by #1.\n3. c10_lang_s020_s025 — Language switching at s020/s025. Paper reports this in Table 8.\n4. gpqa_ds_importance_s015 — GPQA pilot. Paper reports as part of Table 3/5.\n5. gpqa_full_battery — GPQA full: DS+QW × imp × 4 sparsities + 2 random controls (10 runs). Paper reports this.\n6. 14b_scale_validation — QW-14B × MATH-500 × 4 sparsities + 1 random control. Paper reports this.\n7. c5_per_phase_gini_interaction — Gini collapse × II correlation analysis. Paper reports in Table 7.\n\nAll planned experiments appear to be covered in the paper.\n\n## Your Task (R1: Methods + Experiments)\n\nEvaluate:\n1. **Experimental design soundness** — Are the baselines appropriate? Is the factorial design sufficient? Are confounds addressed?\n2. **Statistical methodology** — Are the tests appropriate (Wilcoxon, Bonferroni, bootstrap CI, Cohen's dz)? Any concerns about multiple comparisons, power, or effect size interpretation?\n3. **Phase classification validity** — Median attention entropy split — is this principled or arbitrary? Could results be an artifact of the classification scheme?\n4. **Interaction Intensity metric** — Is II = compound_perplexity_increase - (head_pruning_increase + kv_eviction_increase) well-defined? Concerns about additivity assumption?\n5. **Sample sizes** — n=500 for primary, n=250 for LLaMA, n=50 for GPQA/14B. Are the smaller experiments adequately powered?\n6. **Reproducibility** — Is enough detail provided? Missing hyperparameters?\n7. **Coverage gaps** — Are there obvious experiments that should have been run?\n8. **Internal consistency** — Do the numbers add up? Any contradictions?\n9. **Mechanism evidence strength** — r=0.26 for Gini–II. Is this sufficient to claim a mechanism?\n\nProvide your review in this structure:\n- Summary (2-3 sentences)\n- Strengths (bulleted)\n- Weaknesses (bulleted, with severity: major/minor)\n- Questions for Authors\n- Missing Experiments or Analyses\n- Preliminary Score (1-6) with justification\n- Confidence (1-5)\n","agentId":"a829dc5b13ff7bb01","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have a thorough understanding of the paper. Let me now write the review.\n\n---\n\n## Review: \"Phase-Dependent Amplification of Compound Sparsity in Long-Chain Reasoning\"\n\n### Summary\n\nThis paper investigates how compound sparsity (simultaneous attention head pruning + KV cache eviction) interacts with reasoning phases in long-chain generation. The core finding is that compound degradation severity is not uniform but phase-modulated: information-dense (\"low-sparsity\") reasoning phases suffer disproportionately more super-additive degradation than structured computation phases, and this effect is primarily driven by importance-based eviction (15-17x larger gradient than random). The paper proposes \"importance discrimination collapse\" as a correlational mechanism and validates across two Qwen2-architecture models (n=500), with preliminary LLaMA cross-architecture evidence (n=49).\n\n### Strengths\n\n- **Well-defined metric and factorial design.** The Interaction Index (II) cleanly operationalizes super-additivity, and the 2 models x 2 strategies x 4 sparsity levels design provides systematic coverage. The additive vs. multiplicative null comparison is a good robustness check.\n\n- **Strong statistical methodology for the primary claim.** The paper uses appropriate nonparametric tests (Wilcoxon, permutation), paired per-problem analysis, median-based estimators for heavy-tailed data, Bonferroni correction, and Jonckheere-Terpstra for dose-response. The n=500 replication substantially strengthens the n=50 pilot.\n\n- **Thorough robustness checks.** Segmentation granularity (tercile/quartile/quintile), minimum phase length sensitivity, asymmetric sparsity spot checks, and outlier winsorization all support the primary finding's stability.\n\n- **Honest reporting of negative/mixed results.** The pilot mitigation failing its pre-registered gate, entropy redistribution not replicating on Qwen, LLaMA cross-architecture evidence being inconclusive for strategy interaction, and the crossover being model-dependent are all transparently reported.\n\n- **Phase segmentation validation.** The kappa=0.88 agreement with DeepSeek's explicit <think> markers on GSM8K (n=48) provides meaningful validation that attention-sparsity phases track model-identified reasoning boundaries.\n\n- **Appropriate use of effect sizes.** Cohen's dz alongside p-values prevents over-reliance on significance alone.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n- **(Major) Circularity confound acknowledged but not resolved.** The paper notes (Limitations) that importance-based eviction scores positions using cumulative attention mass *from the pruned model*, creating a circular dependency: head pruning degrades the very scores used for eviction. The entire phase-strategy interaction pattern could be an artifact of this scoring confound rather than a property of \"compound sparsity\" in general. The paper's core claim would be substantially weaker if reframed as \"H2O-style scoring fails gracefully in high-sparsity phases but catastrophically in low-sparsity phases when combined with head pruning.\" Testing even one eviction method with pruning-independent scoring (e.g., recency, learned, or pre-computed static importance) would disambiguate this.\n\n- **(Major) Phase classification is a continuous operationalization treated as a finding.** Segmenting by attention sparsity terciles and then measuring how much *compound sparsity interacts with attention sparsity* is partially circular: tokens with broadly distributed attention (low-sparsity phase) are precisely those where eviction of \"unimportant\" positions matters most, almost by construction. The II asymmetry between phases may be a mathematical consequence of the attention distribution's shape rather than revealing something non-obvious about \"reasoning phases.\" The <think> token validation partially mitigates this but only shows correlation between attention sparsity and reasoning boundaries---not that the phase-dependent II pattern is non-trivial.\n\n- **(Major) Mechanism claim is weak (r=0.26 for Gini-II).** The paper acknowledges that both strategies exhibit \"near-identical Gini drops\" yet severity diverges 15-17x, which means Gini collapse cannot be the mechanism. The \"structural specificity\" argument (importance-pruning removes dispersed-attention heads) is plausible but is presented with only correlational evidence (Mann-Whitney on pruned vs. retained head Gini). There is no demonstrated causal path from Gini collapse to the 15-17x severity gap.\n\n- **(Minor) Cross-architecture evidence is underpowered and somewhat contradicts the narrative.** LLaMA (n=49) shows random eviction *also* has a significant phase gradient (dz 0.35-0.44), which weakens the strategy-specificity claim. The paper frames this as \"architecture-dependent\" but the n=49 sample is insufficient to distinguish \"weak effect we can't detect\" from \"absent effect.\" The 14B results and GPQA results appear as placeholders in the paper (Tables 5/6 show \"---\"), making it unclear what is actually demonstrated vs. planned.\n\n- **(Minor) Sample size heterogeneity weakens secondary claims.** GSM8K (n=50), LLaMA (n=49), GPQA (n=50 in the summary but n=198 in the tex), and the pilot mitigation (n=30) are all substantially underpowered relative to the primary n=500 analysis. The GSM8K random conditions all have |d|<0.164, well below detectability (d>=0.40 needed at n=50). Claiming \"strategy specificity attenuates\" on GSM8K when random is simply undetectable is overinterpretation.\n\n- **(Minor) The \"strategy specificity ratio\" metric conflates detection with magnitude.** Ratios of 15-36x on MATH-500 vs. 2.3-3.8x on GSM8K are computed differently: MATH-500 compares two significant effects, but GSM8K compares a significant effect to a non-significant one. Dividing by a near-zero non-significant denominator does not yield a meaningful ratio.\n\n- **(Minor) Greedy decoding only.** All experiments use greedy decoding (temperature=0). Stochastic decoding would alter attention patterns and potentially the phase structure; the findings may not generalize to sampling-based inference.\n\n- **(Minor) max_new_tokens=2048 for the primary experiment is short.** DeepSeek's reasoning traces frequently truncate even at 8192 tokens (76% truncation at s=0.30). The primary perplexity analysis at 2048 tokens may be capturing truncation-related artifacts in the attention patterns of late-generation tokens approaching the limit.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. Have you considered testing a pruning-independent eviction strategy (e.g., pure recency-based or pre-computed static importance from the dense model) to disentangle the H2O scoring confound from the compound interaction per se?\n\n2. If you define phases by attention sparsity and then show that compound sparsity severity correlates with attention sparsity, how do you distinguish your finding from the tautological statement that \"eviction matters more where attention is dispersed\"?\n\n3. For the Gini mechanism: if both strategies show identical Gini collapse but 15-17x severity difference, what fraction of the severity difference does your \"structural specificity\" account for quantitatively?\n\n4. The per-problem concordance across models is non-significant (rho=-0.107). Does this undermine the claim of a universal \"phase-modulated\" phenomenon, suggesting instead that model-specific attention patterns drive the effect?\n\n5. Why were the 14B and GPQA results left as placeholders in the paper? Are these experiments complete?\n\n### Missing Experiments or Analyses\n\n1. **Pruning-independent eviction control.** Testing recency-based or static-score eviction under the same head pruning would isolate whether the interaction is specific to adaptive importance scoring or general to compound sparsity.\n\n2. **Phase-shuffled null.** Randomly permuting the phase assignments within each problem while preserving token-level II values would test whether the phase-II correlation exceeds chance beyond what the attention-sparsity/II relationship mechanically guarantees.\n\n3. **Per-token regression analysis.** Regressing II on continuous attention sparsity (rather than discretizing into phases) would reveal whether the tercile boundaries are special or if this is a smooth monotonic relationship.\n\n4. **Larger model validation.** The 14B and GPQA experiments are incomplete (placeholders). For a paper claiming generality of phase modulation, these should be filled in.\n\n5. **Multiple random seeds.** Random eviction uses a single random draw per condition. Confidence intervals on random eviction should account for seed variability.\n\n### Preliminary Score: 4 (Weak Accept)\n\n**Justification:** The primary finding---that compound sparsity severity concentrates in low-attention-sparsity phases under importance-based eviction---is well-supported statistically at n=500 with appropriate methodology. The paper is thorough in its robustness checks and honest about limitations. However, the circularity concern (importance scoring confound) and the partial tautology in the phase classification substantially limit the novelty of the insight: \"importance-based eviction, which relies on attention scores, fails more where attention is dispersed\" is less surprising than the framing suggests. The mechanism claim (discrimination collapse) is acknowledged as correlational and quantitatively insufficient (r=0.26, identical Gini drops across strategies). The incomplete tables (14B, GPQA) weaken the claimed scope. Overall, this is a solid empirical characterization with useful observations for the KV cache compression community, but the conceptual novelty is limited by the confound structure, and the generalization claims are not yet fully supported.\n\n### Confidence: 4/5\n\nI have substantial expertise in transformer compression and statistical methodology. My main uncertainty is whether the GPQA/14B experiments (described in the review context but showing as placeholders in the actual tex) should be evaluated as \"demonstrated\" or \"planned.\" If demonstrated, the paper is somewhat stronger on generalization; if not, the scope claims are not adequately supported."}],"totalDurationMs":120693,"totalTokens":73574,"totalToolUseCount":21,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3006,"cache_read_input_tokens":68198,"output_tokens":2369,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3006},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":2369,"cache_read_input_tokens":68198,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3006,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3006,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":14,"searchCount":2,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":5}}}
